<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir:&#10;/REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/05-生命手性/full&#10;Topic: Research question: Why does life require chirality?&#10;Context: Molecules in DNA and RNA have the interesting&#10;feature of “handedness,” also called chirality. While&#10;these molecules naturally appear in both left- and right-&#10;handed forms, only the right-handed molecules are found in&#10;living organisms. “Handedness serves an essential function&#10;in living beings,” writes Emily Singer in Quanta. “Many of&#10;the chemical reactions that drive our cells only work with&#10;molecules of the correct handedness.” Many scientists&#10;believe that chirality is an essential component of&#10;biochemistry, given that it affects how cellular elements&#10;interact with each other. Molecular locks require a&#10;molecule of the correct handedness to open them. But it is&#10;still unclear whether chirality is a defining requirement&#10;for life, especially if mankind formulates different&#10;definitions of “life” in the future.&#10;Status: ok&#10;Failed step: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="510" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: Why does life require chirality?&#10;Context: Molecules in DNA and RNA have the interesting&#10;feature of “handedness,” also called chirality. While&#10;these molecules naturally appear in both left- and right-&#10;handed forms, only the right-handed molecules are found in&#10;living organisms. “Handedness serves an essential function&#10;in living beings,” writes Emily Singer in Quanta. “Many of&#10;the chemical reactions that drive our cells only work with&#10;molecules of the correct handedness.” Many scientists&#10;believe that chirality is an essential component of&#10;biochemistry, given that it affects how cellular elements&#10;interact with each other. Molecular locks require a&#10;molecule of the correct handedness to open them. But it is&#10;still unclear whether chirality is a defining requirement&#10;for life, especially if mankind formulates different&#10;definitions of “life” in the future.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1230" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: completed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Examining the Effects of&#10;Homochirality for Electron&#10;Transfer in Protein Assemblies&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4384627421&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Emergence of homochirality in&#10;large molecular systems&#10;year: 2021&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3120596068&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="148" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Origin of Homochirality: The&#10;Formation and Stability of&#10;Homochiral Peptides in Aqueous&#10;Prebiological Environment in the&#10;Earth’s Crust&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4313574776&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Structural Contributions to&#10;Autocatalysis and Asymmetric&#10;Amplification in the Soai Reaction&#10;year: 2020&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3210500841&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 6292&#10;trend_chars: 7891" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: Why does life require&#10;chirality? Context: Molecules in DNA and RNA have the&#10;interesting feature of “handedness,” also called chirality.&#10;While these molecules naturally appear in both left- and&#10;right-handed forms, only the right-handed molecules are found&#10;in living organisms. “Handedness serves an essential function&#10;in living beings,” writes Emily Singer in Quanta. “Many of the&#10;chemical reactions that drive our cells only work with&#10;molecules of the correct handedness.” Many scientists believe&#10;that chirality is an essential component of biochemistry,&#10;given that it affects how cellular elements interact with each&#10;other. Molecular locks require a molecule of the correct&#10;handedness to open them. But it is still unclear whether&#10;chirality is a defining requirement for life, especially if&#10;mankind formulates different definitions of “life” in the&#10;future. seed_paper: Examining the Effects of Homochirality for&#10;Electron Transfer in Protein Assemblies # CITATION SUBGRAPH (3&#10;refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1] Emergence of&#10;homochirality in large molecular systems (2021, venue=N/A)&#10;authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = []&#10;cite_count = 56 section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown&#10;is_influential = False low_confidence = True cited_by_subgraph&#10;= 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[] delta_yr=unknown&#10;edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: The selection of a single&#10;molecular handedness, or homochirality across all living&#10;matter, is a mystery in the origin of life. Frank's seminal&#10;model showed in the '50s how chiral symmetry breaking can&#10;occur in nonequilibrium chemical networks. However, an&#10;important shortcoming in this classic model Existing Methods:&#10;Unavailable from local metadata. Motivation: The selection of&#10;a single molecular handedness, or homochirality across all&#10;living matter, is a mystery in the origin of life. Frank's&#10;seminal model showed in the '50s how chiral symmetry breaking&#10;can occur in nonequilibrium chemical networks. However, an&#10;important shortcoming in this classic model Proposed Method:&#10;The selection of a single molecular handedness, or&#10;homochirality across all living matter, is a mystery in the&#10;origin of life. Frank's seminal model showed in the '50s how&#10;chiral symmetry breaking can occur in nonequilibrium chemical&#10;networks. However, an important shortcoming in this classic&#10;model Experiment Plan: Unavailable from local metadata.&#10;[ABSTRACT] The selection of a single molecular handedness, or&#10;homochirality across all living matter, is a mystery in the&#10;origin of life. Frank's seminal model showed in the '50s how&#10;chiral symmetry breaking can occur in nonequilibrium chemical&#10;networks. However, an important shortcoming in this classic&#10;model is that it consid... ## [2] Origin of Homochirality: The&#10;Formation and Stability of Homochiral Peptides in Aqueous&#10;Prebiological Environment in the Earth’s Crust (2023,&#10;venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth = 1&#10;cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 6 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = False low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 0 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: The oldest&#10;forms of living org...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Using the temporal order implied&#10;by publication years, Paper 0 is the 2020 work on the Soai&#10;reaction, *“Structural Contributions to Autocatalysis and&#10;Asymmetric Amplification in the Soai Reaction.”* This paper&#10;treats chirality primarily as a mechanistic chemical problem:&#10;how a small enantiomeric imbalance can be amplified through&#10;autocatalysis, mixed catalyst–substrate states, and structural&#10;feedback in a specific organic reaction. Its central&#10;contribution is to show that strong asymmetric amplification&#10;is chemically possible, but it remains tied to a specialized&#10;reaction system. Paper 1, the 2021 work *“Emergence of&#10;Homochirality in Large Molecular Systems,”* moves the question&#10;from a single reaction to system-level chiral symmetry&#10;breaking. It invokes Frank-type nonequilibrium network models&#10;and addresses their limitations when applied to large&#10;molecular systems. The transition is therefore from “how can&#10;one reaction amplify chirality?” to “how can many interacting&#10;molecular components collectively select one handedness?” This&#10;is a key conceptual advance because biological homochirality&#10;is not a property of one molecule but of coupled biochemical&#10;networks. Paper 1 to Paper 2: Paper 2, the 2023 work *“Origin&#10;of Homochirality: The Formation and Stability of Homochiral&#10;Peptides in Aqueous Prebiological Environment in the Earth’s&#10;Crust,”* shifts the emphasis from abstract network models to&#10;geochemically plausible origin-of-life settings. It asks&#10;whether homochiral peptides could form and remain stable in&#10;aqueous crustal or hydrothermal environments, where flow,&#10;dilution, racemization, and chemical complexity could oppose&#10;chiral selection. The advance is environmental and biochemical&#10;realism: rather than asking only whether homochirality can&#10;emerge in principle, it asks whether homochiral molecular&#10;assemblies could persist under conditions relevant to early&#10;Earth. This moves the field closer to the biological question&#10;by connecting chiral emergence to peptides, prebiotic self-&#10;assembly, and habitats associated with early life. Key Turning&#10;Points: 1. From asymmetric amplification to network-level&#10;homochirality. The Soai reaction demonstrates that&#10;autocatalytic chemistry can amplify chirality, but the 2021&#10;paper marks a turn toward explaining how such amplification&#10;can operate across large molecular systems. This is a shift&#10;from isol..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2022" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3090" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Origin-of-life biochemistry and prebiotic&#10;chemistry concerned with why biological systems require&#10;homochirality, including chiral symmetry breaking, molecular&#10;recognition, peptide/protein assembly function, and electron&#10;transfer.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing work explains chiral amplification in&#10;specialized reactions, network-level symmetry breaking, and&#10;partial prebiotic stability of homochiral peptides, but it&#10;does not provide a general quantitative mechanism linking&#10;homochirality to life-required functions such as electron&#10;transfer, molecular recognition, catalytic specificity,&#10;replication fidelity, and cross-polymer coordination, nor does&#10;it establish whether chirality is universally necessary or&#10;contingent under alternative definitions of life.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- molecular handedness&#10;- enantiomers&#10;- homochirality&#10;- racemic or near-racemic mixtures&#10;- chiral symmetry breaking&#10;- asymmetric autocatalysis&#10;- Soai reaction&#10;- Frank-type nonequilibrium network models&#10;- large molecular systems&#10;- homochiral peptides&#10;- protein assemblies&#10;- DNA and RNA handedness&#10;- amino acids&#10;- sugars&#10;- nucleic acids&#10;- lipids&#10;- aqueous prebiological environments&#10;- Earth crust and hydrothermal settings&#10;- molecular recognition&#10;- molecular lock-key compatibility&#10;- electron transfer&#10;- catalytic specificity&#10;- replication fidelity&#10;- protocellular life&#10;- alternative definitions of life&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- small enantiomeric imbalances can be amplified by asymmetric&#10;autocatalysis&#10;- nonequilibrium chemical networks can produce chiral symmetry&#10;breaking&#10;- large molecular systems constrain the robustness of chiral&#10;symmetry breaking&#10;- aqueous crustal and hydrothermal conditions affect the&#10;formation and stability of homochiral peptides&#10;- homochirality constrains molecular recognition through&#10;handedness-specific compatibility&#10;- homochirality may influence electron transfer efficiency in&#10;protein assemblies&#10;- chiral amplification must be connected to life-relevant&#10;molecular functions&#10;- separate chiral selections in different molecular classes&#10;must become coordinated for coherent biochemistry&#10;- the necessity of chirality may depend on how life is defined&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- transition from racemic or weakly biased mixtures to&#10;decisive homochirality remains difficult without special&#10;assumptions&#10;- models often assume controlled nonequilibrium conditions&#10;that are not fully compatible with flow, dilution,&#10;racemization, mineral surfaces, temperature gradients, and&#10;competing reactions&#10;- the Soai reaction demonstrates asymmetric amplification but&#10;is chemically specialized and not clearly generalizable to&#10;biology&#10;- peptide-focused prebiotic scenarios do not yet explain&#10;coordinated homochirality across amino acids, sugars, nucleic&#10;acids, and lipids&#10;- the functional advantage of homochirality is not&#10;quantitatively established for electron transfer, catalysis,&#10;error suppression, or information fidelity&#10;- there is no general metric for deciding whether chirality is&#10;a universal requirement for life or a contingent feature of&#10;terrestrial biochemistry&#10;- the route from chiral amplification to protocellular&#10;organization, heredity, and evolution remains incomplete&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- homochirality as universal life requirement versus frozen&#10;accident of terrestrial biology&#10;- chemical emergence of chirality versus biological functional&#10;necessity of chirality&#10;- idealized symmetry-breaking models versus messy prebiotic&#10;environmental realism&#10;- specialized autocatalytic reactions versus coordinated&#10;multi-polymer biochemistry&#10;- racemic or mixed-handed alternative life possibilities&#10;versus obligate homochirality in known life&#10;- prebiotic selection for chirality versus neutral or&#10;contingent fixation before Darwinian evolution&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- provide a physical-chemical mechanism linking homochirality&#10;to life-relevant functions&#10;- quantify advantages of homochirality for electron transfer,&#10;molecular recognition, catalysis, stability, replication&#10;fidelity, or error suppression&#10;- operate from racemic or weakly biased initial conditions&#10;without implausible special assumptions&#10;- remain robust under prebiotically plausible conditions such&#10;as flow, dilution, racemization, competing reactions, and&#10;environmental gradients&#10;- generalize beyond a single reaction or single molecular&#10;class&#10;- explain coordination among chiral biomolecular classes such&#10;as amino acids, sugars, nucleic acids, and lipids&#10;- connect chiral emergence to protocellular organization,&#10;metabolism, heredity, or evolution&#10;- provide criteria for assessing whether chirality is&#10;necessary, advantageous, or contingent under different&#10;definitions of life&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- nonequilibrium symmetry-breaking feedback&#10;- autocatalytic asymmetric amplification&#10;- environmental selection through gradients, confinement,&#10;flow, or mineral surfaces&#10;- chiral molecular recognition or lock-key filtering&#10;- stability selection of homochiral polymers or assemblies&#10;- electron-transfer optimization through chiral structural&#10;order&#10;- cross-catalytic coupling between different chiral molecular&#10;classes&#10;- compartmentalized amplification and selection&#10;- error suppression or information-fidelity selection&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- Frank-type nonequilibrium reaction network models&#10;- asymmetric autocatalysis and chiral amplification chemistry&#10;- prebiotic geochemistry in aqueous crustal and hydrothermal&#10;environments&#10;- peptide and protein assembly electron transfer&#10;- handedness-dependent molecular recognition and lock-key&#10;binding&#10;- polymer stability and self-assembly under racemic or mixed-&#10;chirality conditions&#10;- protocell compartmentalization and selection before&#10;Darwinian evolution&#10;- thermodynamic and kinetic theories of prebiotic selection&#10;- alternative life definitions and non-terrestrial or&#10;synthetic biochemical contexts" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3090" width="560" height="2832" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6022" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to remain grounded&#10;in chiral symmetry breaking, asymmetric autocatalysis,&#10;prebiotic peptide chemistry, and origin-of-life network&#10;models. Cross-domain retrieval is also justified because the&#10;central bottlenecks require mechanism transfer: quantitative&#10;links from homochirality to electron transfer, molecular&#10;recognition, catalytic specificity, replication fidelity,&#10;cross-polymer coordination, and life-definition criteria are&#10;not resolved within the existing subgraph." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6022" width="560" height="312" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6434" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#